window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["az-900"]=[{"id":"az-900-f0001","card":"q-az-900-001-1-exam-scope-and-terminology-updates","sec":"az-900-s002","q":"What is Microsoft Entra ID, and how does it relate to the older name Azure Active Directory (Azure AD)?","a":"<p>Microsoft Entra ID is the new name of Azure Active Directory. It is the same cloud identity service, and the CLI and PowerShell commands (for example <code>az ad user list</code>) did not change. Microsoft Entra is the product family, and Entra ID is one product inside it. Naming note: older docs, courses and exam material still say \"Azure AD\", so you need to recognize both names.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0002","card":"q-az-900-001-1-exam-scope-and-terminology-updates","sec":"az-900-s002","q":"What was Azure AD Domain Services renamed to, and what does it provide?","a":"<p>It is now Microsoft Entra Domain Services. It is a managed Windows Active Directory domain in the cloud that supports LDAP, Kerberos and NTLM, so legacy apps can use domain join and group policy without you running domain controllers. Naming note: this is the current name, but check the docs because Microsoft renames products often.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0003","card":"q-az-900-001-1-exam-scope-and-terminology-updates","sec":"az-900-s002","q":"What does \"serverless\" mean in cloud computing, and does it mean there are no servers?","a":"<p>Servers still exist, but the provider provisions, scales and manages them. You only write and deploy code (for example an Azure Function) and you are billed per execution or resource consumed. The trade-off is less control over the runtime and possible cold-start latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0004","card":"q-az-900-001-1-exam-scope-and-terminology-updates","sec":"az-900-s002","q":"In AZ-900, which four areas of knowledge does the exam cover, and where do IaC and serverless fit?","a":"<p>Cloud concepts, Azure architecture and services, identity/access/security, and management and governance. Serverless belongs to cloud concepts, and Infrastructure as Code belongs to managing and deploying resources. Exam outlines change over time, so check the current skills outline before you study.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0005","card":"q-az-900-002-2-cloud-concepts-models-and-shared-respo","sec":"az-900-s003","q":"What problem does cloud computing solve compared with running your own data center?","a":"<p>It replaces upfront hardware purchases and long procurement with on-demand, pay-as-you-go resources. You get quick provisioning, scaling in both directions, built-in redundancy, and tools for monitoring and cost control. The trade-off is less control over the physical layer and ongoing cost that must be managed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0006","card":"q-az-900-002-2-cloud-concepts-models-and-shared-respo","sec":"az-900-s003","q":"Name the main benefits of the public cloud and the main drawbacks.","a":"<p>Benefits: agility, fast deployment, pay-as-you-go, easy management and cost-tracking tools. Drawbacks: less control and flexibility, regulatory limits for some data, and your workloads run on shared (multi-tenant) infrastructure, although tenants are logically isolated.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0007","card":"q-az-900-003-shared-responsibility-model","sec":"az-900-s004","q":"What is the shared responsibility model in cloud computing?","a":"<p>The provider and the customer each secure part of the stack, and the provider never takes all of it. The provider covers what it controls (physical data centers, hosts, network fabric). You always keep responsibility for your data, your identities and access, and your own application code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0008","card":"q-az-900-003-shared-responsibility-model","sec":"az-900-s004","q":"How does choosing IaaS, PaaS or SaaS change what you are responsible for?","a":"<p>Moving from IaaS to PaaS to SaaS shifts more of the stack to the provider. With IaaS you patch the OS and manage runtime and apps. With PaaS you manage only the app and data. With SaaS you mostly manage your data, user access and configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0009","card":"q-az-900-003-shared-responsibility-model","sec":"az-900-s004","q":"A bug in your web app leaks customer data while it runs on a fully managed PaaS service. Who is responsible and why?","a":"<p>You are. The provider secures the platform it controls (hardware, OS, runtime patching), but application logic, data handling and access control belong to the customer in every service model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0010","card":"q-az-900-004-cloud-models","sec":"az-900-s005","q":"What is the difference between public, private and hybrid cloud?","a":"<p>Public cloud is shared provider infrastructure (multi-tenant) that you rent. Private cloud is dedicated to one organization (single-tenant), either self-owned or hosted by a third party. Hybrid cloud combines a private or on-premises environment with a public cloud and connects them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0011","card":"q-az-900-004-cloud-models","sec":"az-900-s005","q":"When would you choose a private cloud over the public cloud?","a":"<p>Choose private when you need strict privacy or regulatory control, full control over the environment, support for legacy systems, or the ability to run without an internet connection (for example a ship that syncs later). The trade-off is high upfront cost for hardware, staff and power, plus the management overhead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0012","card":"q-az-900-004-cloud-models","sec":"az-900-s005","q":"What makes hybrid cloud hard to run, and when is it still the right choice?","a":"<p>Setup and troubleshooting are complex, data must stay compatible with older systems, and the team needs strong IT skills. It is the right choice when sensitive systems (for example a database) must stay on-premises or legacy systems must keep running, while you still want public cloud scale for other workloads.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0013","card":"q-az-900-005-cloud-models","sec":"az-900-s005","q":"How do you choose a cloud model for a new project?","a":"<p>List the requirements first (management effort, privacy, security, cost, control, connectivity), then test each model against them. Public wins on low cost and quick deployment. Private wins on control and strict compliance. Hybrid fits when you need both legacy or sensitive systems on-premises and cloud elasticity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0014","card":"q-az-900-005-cloud-models","sec":"az-900-s005","q":"Which Azure product runs Azure services on hardware you host yourself, and what cloud model does it support?","a":"<p>Azure Stack HCI (now part of Azure Local, a naming change, so check the current docs) runs Azure-connected services on your own or a partner's hardware. It supports private and hybrid designs. Azure Arc is the related tool for managing such resources from Azure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0015","card":"q-az-900-006-consumption-based-model","sec":"az-900-s006","q":"What is the consumption-based pricing model, and does an idle VM cost money?","a":"<p>You pay for the resources you use or have allocated rather than a flat upfront purchase. A VM that is running but idle is still billed for its size, because the compute is reserved for you. Stop and deallocate it to stop the compute charge.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0016","card":"q-az-900-006-consumption-based-model","sec":"az-900-s006","q":"What are the two rules for controlling cost in a consumption model?","a":"<p>Do not allocate more than you need (right-size, for example 2 cores instead of 8), and make full use of what you allocate (do not leave resources idle). Autoscaling, cost alerts and Azure Advisor recommendations support both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0017","card":"q-az-900-006-consumption-based-model","sec":"az-900-s006","q":"What is the difference between <code>az vm stop</code> and <code>az vm deallocate</code> for billing?","a":"<p><code>az vm stop</code> shuts down the OS but keeps the compute allocated, so you still pay for the VM size. <code>az vm deallocate</code> releases the compute, so the compute charge stops. Disks and reserved public IPs still cost money in both cases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0018","card":"q-az-900-007-high-availability","sec":"az-900-s007","q":"What is high availability, and how do you achieve it in the cloud?","a":"<p>High availability means the app stays accessible to users at an agreed level, commonly 99% or more. You get it by removing single points of failure with redundancy: multiple instances, availability zones or availability sets, load balancing, and redundant storage and network paths.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0019","card":"q-az-900-007-high-availability","sec":"az-900-s007","q":"What does an SLA of 99.9% versus 99.99% mean in downtime per year?","a":"<p>99.9% allows about 8.76 hours of downtime a year, and 99.99% allows about 52.6 minutes. Each extra nine is much harder and costlier to reach, so choose the SLA the business actually needs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0020","card":"q-az-900-007-high-availability","sec":"az-900-s007","q":"What kinds of failures threaten availability that you should design for?","a":"<p>Network outages, application failures, VM or host failures, power outages, and the failure of a dependency such as a database server. Redundancy at each layer limits the effect of any one failure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0021","card":"q-az-900-007-high-availability","sec":"az-900-s007","q":"How does the SLA of a solution change when it depends on several services in a chain?","a":"<p>The composite SLA is the product of the individual SLAs, so it is lower than the weakest single service. For example two services at 99.9% give about 99.8%. Adding redundant instances in parallel raises availability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0022","card":"q-az-900-008-scalability","sec":"az-900-s008","q":"What is the difference between scaling up and scaling out?","a":"<p>Scale up (vertical) moves to a bigger machine with more CPU or memory. Scale out (horizontal) adds more identical instances. Scaling out needs a load balancer and an app that handles multiple instances, but it has no single-machine ceiling and can improve availability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0023","card":"q-az-900-008-scalability","sec":"az-900-s008","q":"Which is cheaper and safer for a web tier that must handle traffic spikes, scale up or scale out, and why?","a":"<p>Scale out is usually better. You add instances only during the spike and remove them afterward (scale in), and losing one instance does not take the app down. Scale up often needs a restart or resize, hits an upper VM size limit, and keeps a single point of failure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0024","card":"q-az-900-008-scalability","sec":"az-900-s008","q":"What is the difference between agility and elasticity?","a":"<p>Agility is how easily and quickly you add or remove resources. Elasticity is automatic scaling with demand in both directions, like a rubber band, so you do not over-provision at quiet times or run short at peak.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0025","card":"q-az-900-009-scalability","sec":"az-900-s008","q":"What is an Azure Virtual Machine Scale Set (VMSS) and when would you use one?","a":"<p>A VMSS manages a group of identical VMs that can scale in and out manually or by autoscale rules, usually behind a load balancer. Use it for stateless, horizontally scalable workloads such as web or API tiers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0026","card":"q-az-900-009-scalability","sec":"az-900-s008","q":"In Azure autoscale, how do you define a rule that scales out when CPU is high?","a":"<p>Create an autoscale setting with minimum, maximum and default instance counts, then add a rule such as <code>Percentage CPU &gt; 70 avg 5m</code> with the action scale out by 2. Add a matching scale-in rule (for example CPU under 30%) so it also shrinks. Using different thresholds for out and in avoids flapping.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0027","card":"q-az-900-009-scalability","sec":"az-900-s008","q":"Why does an autoscale setting need both a minimum and a maximum instance count?","a":"<p>The minimum guarantees baseline capacity and availability, and the maximum caps cost if a metric spikes or a bug causes runaway load. Autoscale only moves within that range.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0028","card":"q-az-900-009-scalability","sec":"az-900-s008","q":"How do you resize a single Azure VM, and what is the catch?","a":"<p>Use <code>az vm resize</code> (or the portal) to choose a new size, and the size must be available in that region and hardware cluster. Resizing normally restarts the VM, so it causes brief downtime, which is why scale out is preferred for availability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0029","card":"q-az-900-010-reliability-and-predictability","sec":"az-900-s009","q":"What is the difference between fault tolerance and disaster recovery?","a":"<p>Fault tolerance keeps the app running through a local fault such as a rack, power or hardware failure, usually with redundancy inside a region (zones, availability sets, ZRS). Disaster recovery restores service after a large event such as a regional outage, using another region (region pairs, GRS, Site Recovery).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0030","card":"q-az-900-010-reliability-and-predictability","sec":"az-900-s009","q":"What do Azure Site Recovery and Azure Backup each do?","a":"<p>Site Recovery replicates VMs to another region so you can fail over during a disaster, which keeps recovery time low. Azure Backup takes scheduled backups into a vault so you can restore data after deletion, corruption or ransomware. Replication is not a backup, because corruption replicates too.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0031","card":"q-az-900-010-reliability-and-predictability","sec":"az-900-s009","q":"What does BCDR stand for and what does a plan define?","a":"<p>Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery. A plan sets how the business keeps operating and recovers after a disaster, usually defined by the recovery time objective (how fast you must be back) and recovery point objective (how much data loss is acceptable).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0032","card":"q-az-900-011-security-and-governance","sec":"az-900-s010","q":"What is the difference between security and governance in the cloud?","a":"<p>Security restricts access to authorized people and protects resources from threats. Governance defines who can do what and how, driven by regulation, company policy and cost control. Azure tools: RBAC and Defender for Cloud for security, Policy, locks and cost management for governance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0033","card":"q-az-900-011-security-and-governance","sec":"az-900-s010","q":"How would you allow only SSH from one IP range to a VM and block all other inbound traffic?","a":"<p>Attach a network security group to the subnet or NIC with an Allow rule for TCP port 22 from that source range, at a low priority number. NSG rules run in priority order, lowest number first, and the first match wins. A custom deny-all rule with a higher number is optional because default rules already deny inbound internet traffic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0034","card":"q-az-900-012-manageability","sec":"az-900-s011","q":"What does manageability mean in the cloud, and which tools support it?","a":"<p>It means you can monitor, scale, deploy and control cost of resources in a managed way. Azure supports it with Azure Monitor and alerts, autoscale, budgets and cost alerts, and multiple deployment routes (portal, CLI, PowerShell, SDKs, ARM templates).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0035","card":"q-az-900-012-manageability","sec":"az-900-s011","q":"How would you get notified before a cloud bill grows beyond plan?","a":"<p>Create a budget in Cost Management with a monthly amount and alert thresholds (for example 80% and 100%), and attach email or an action group. A budget notifies but does not stop resources by default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0036","card":"q-az-900-013-4-service-types-iaas-paas-saas","sec":"az-900-s012","q":"What are the differences between IaaS, PaaS and SaaS in who manages what?","a":"<p>IaaS: the provider manages hardware, network and storage, and you manage OS, middleware, runtime, apps and data. PaaS: the provider also manages OS and runtime, and you manage app and data. SaaS: the provider manages everything and you manage only your data and configuration. Control falls and ease of use rises from IaaS to SaaS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0037","card":"q-az-900-013-4-service-types-iaas-paas-saas","sec":"az-900-s012","q":"Give an Azure example of IaaS, PaaS and SaaS.","a":"<p>IaaS: Azure Virtual Machines. PaaS: Azure App Service. SaaS: Microsoft 365 (also Outlook.com or OneDrive from the Microsoft family).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0038","card":"q-az-900-013-4-service-types-iaas-paas-saas","sec":"az-900-s012","q":"What are the trade-offs of IaaS compared with PaaS?","a":"<p>IaaS gives the most control and flexibility (any OS, any software, custom config) and suits lift-and-shift. The price is the highest management burden: patching, security, scaling, monitoring and backups are on you. PaaS removes that burden but limits you to supported runtimes and platform choices.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0039","card":"q-az-900-014-4-service-types-iaas-paas-saas","sec":"az-900-s012","q":"For a VM, which tasks remain your responsibility?","a":"<p>OS updates and patching, installing and configuring software, opening and restricting ports, monitoring, scaling and backups. Azure only manages the physical host, network and virtualization layer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0040","card":"q-az-900-014-4-service-types-iaas-paas-saas","sec":"az-900-s012","q":"What turnkey features does Azure App Service give you that a VM does not?","a":"<p>Built-in autoscale, custom domains, TLS/SSL certificate binding, deployment slots, continuous deployment from GitHub, and built-in sign-in with identity providers. You turn them on and configure them instead of installing and managing the software yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0041","card":"q-az-900-014-4-service-types-iaas-paas-saas","sec":"az-900-s012","q":"When would you choose PaaS over IaaS for a web application?","a":"<p>Choose PaaS when you want to focus on the app, not on OS patching and infrastructure, and your app runs on a supported runtime. Choose IaaS when you need OS-level control, custom software, or an unsupported runtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0042","card":"q-az-900-015-4-service-types-iaas-paas-saas","sec":"az-900-s012","q":"How do you work with a SaaS product such as Microsoft 365 programmatically?","a":"<p>You do not deploy anything. You call its API, for example Microsoft Graph with an OAuth bearer token (<code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>), to read the signed-in user's profile or mail. The provider runs and updates the software.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0043","card":"q-az-900-015-4-service-types-iaas-paas-saas","sec":"az-900-s012","q":"When is SaaS the right choice, and what is the main drawback?","a":"<p>Choose SaaS when you just need to use standard software (email, office tools, CRM) with the least management. The drawback is the least control and customization, and dependence on the vendor's features and roadmap.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0044","card":"q-az-900-015-4-service-types-iaas-paas-saas","sec":"az-900-s012","q":"Which service model is Azure Functions, and how is it billed?","a":"<p>It is serverless. You supply code only, an event triggers it, and on the Consumption plan you pay per execution and resource use, with no VM cost while idle.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0045","card":"q-az-900-016-thought-experiment-choosing-a-cloud-mode","sec":"az-900-s013","q":"A company needs VMs and a database, has few network engineers, must never share a network with unknown tenants, and has a generous budget. Which cloud model fits and why?","a":"<p>A private cloud hosted by a vetted third-party provider. Single-tenant infrastructure meets the privacy rule, the provider handles the networking, and scaling and fault tolerance are available. Public fails the privacy rule. Hybrid needs complex on-premises-to-cloud networking that the small team cannot run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0046","card":"q-az-900-016-thought-experiment-choosing-a-cloud-mode","sec":"az-900-s013","q":"Why should you list requirements before picking a cloud model?","a":"<p>Each model scores differently on cost, control, privacy, speed and skills. Testing each against explicit requirements shows trade-offs, such as accepting higher cost to meet a privacy or compliance rule.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0047","card":"q-az-900-017-geographies-regions-region-pairs","sec":"az-900-s014","q":"What are an Azure geography, a region and a region pair?","a":"<p>A geography is a boundary (country, area or regulatory zone) that keeps data in compliance with local rules. A region is a set of data centers within a geography. A region pair links two regions in the same geography, at least about 300 miles apart, for disaster recovery.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0048","card":"q-az-900-017-geographies-regions-region-pairs","sec":"az-900-s014","q":"What benefits do region pairs give?","a":"<p>If a regional outage hits, replicated resources can fail over to the paired region (for example with geo-redundant storage). Microsoft also updates one region of a pair at a time and verifies it before the other, which limits update-related downtime. Recovery in an outage prioritizes one region of each pair.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0049","card":"q-az-900-017-geographies-regions-region-pairs","sec":"az-900-s014","q":"How do you pick a region for a workload?","a":"<p>Consider data residency and compliance rules, latency to your users, available services and VM sizes in that region, price (varies by region) and whether the region has availability zones and a pair for DR.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0050","card":"q-az-900-018-sovereign-regions","sec":"az-900-s015","q":"What is a sovereign region in Azure, and why does it exist?","a":"<p>It is an isolated instance of Azure with separate portals, endpoints and operators, built to meet legal or compliance needs of a government or country. Examples: Azure Government (US) and Azure China, which is run by 21Vianet.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0051","card":"q-az-900-018-sovereign-regions","sec":"az-900-s015","q":"Who can use Azure Government (US)?","a":"<p>US federal, state, local and tribal governments and eligible partners, with data kept in the US and operated by screened US personnel. A subset serves the Department of Defense with dedicated data centers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0052","card":"q-az-900-018-sovereign-regions","sec":"az-900-s015","q":"How do you point the Azure CLI at a sovereign cloud?","a":"<p>Run <code>az cloud set --name AzureUSGovernment</code> (or <code>AzureChinaCloud</code>) and then <code>az login</code>. This changes the endpoints the CLI uses, because sovereign clouds are separate from public Azure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0053","card":"q-az-900-018-sovereign-regions","sec":"az-900-s015","q":"What happened to Azure Germany?","a":"<p>Version note: Azure Germany (operated by T-Systems as data trustee) was closed to new customers and retired in 2021, because Microsoft's standard German regions now meet the same data residency needs. Check the docs for the current list of sovereign clouds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0054","card":"q-az-900-019-availability-zones","sec":"az-900-s016","q":"What is an availability zone, and how many does a zone-enabled region have?","a":"<p>An availability zone is a physically separate location inside one region with its own power, cooling and networking, made of one or more data centers. Zone-enabled regions have at least three zones, and not every region supports zones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0055","card":"q-az-900-019-availability-zones","sec":"az-900-s016","q":"What is the difference between zonal and zone-redundant services?","a":"<p>With a zonal service you pick the zone yourself and handle replication (for example a VM pinned to zone 1). With a zone-redundant service Azure replicates across zones for you (for example zone-redundant storage).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0056","card":"q-az-900-019-availability-zones","sec":"az-900-s016","q":"Do availability zones protect you from a regional disaster?","a":"<p>No. All zones sit in the same region, so a regional event can take them all down. Zones give fault tolerance for data center failures, and you need a second region (region pair) for disaster recovery.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0057","card":"q-az-900-019-availability-zones","sec":"az-900-s016","q":"How do you place a VM in an availability zone, and what do you need to do for real resilience?","a":"<p>Set the zone on the VM, for example <code>az vm create ... --zone 1</code>. For resilience, deploy VMs in at least two zones and put a zone-redundant load balancer in front, because a single VM in one zone still fails with that zone.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0058","card":"q-az-900-020-data-centers","sec":"az-900-s017","q":"What is an Azure data center and why does one failure not affect others?","a":"<p>It is a physical building with its own power, generators, cooling and networking. Data centers do not share these resources, so a failure in one does not cascade, and Microsoft owns or exclusively leases the connecting cables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0059","card":"q-az-900-021-resources-resource-groups-management-gro","sec":"az-900-s018","q":"What is the Azure resource hierarchy from top to bottom?","a":"<p>Management groups, then subscriptions, then resource groups, then resources. Settings such as policy and RBAC assigned at a higher level are inherited by every child scope under it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0060","card":"q-az-900-021-resources-resource-groups-management-gro","sec":"az-900-s018","q":"What are the rules and benefits of a resource group?","a":"<p>Every resource belongs to exactly one resource group. A group lets you view costs together, apply tags, assign RBAC and locks at group level, export a template, and delete everything in one step. Resources in a group may be in different regions, and group metadata lives in the group's own region.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0061","card":"q-az-900-021-resources-resource-groups-management-gro","sec":"az-900-s018","q":"What can a management group contain, and why use one?","a":"<p>It can contain subscriptions and other (nested) management groups, but not resource groups or resources directly. Use it to apply Policy and RBAC across many subscriptions, for example by department or environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0062","card":"q-az-900-021-resources-resource-groups-management-gro","sec":"az-900-s018","q":"How should you structure resource groups for a project?","a":"<p>Group resources that share a lifecycle (deployed, managed and deleted together), for example one group per application environment. Do not mix long-lived shared resources with short-lived test resources, because deleting a group removes everything inside.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0063","card":"q-az-900-022-6-compute-and-networking","sec":"az-900-s019","q":"What is compute in Azure, and what are the main compute options?","a":"<p>Compute is any service that consumes CPU and memory to run your code. The main options are Virtual Machines (IaaS), Container Instances and AKS for containers, App Service for web apps (PaaS), and Azure Functions (serverless).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0064","card":"q-az-900-022-6-compute-and-networking","sec":"az-900-s019","q":"How do you choose between VMs, containers and Functions?","a":"<p>Pick VMs for full OS control and lift-and-shift. Pick containers for portable, quickly started apps with packaged dependencies. Pick Functions for short, event-driven code where you want no server management and pay per execution.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0065","card":"q-az-900-023-containers","sec":"az-900-s020","q":"What is a container and how is it different from a virtual machine?","a":"<p>A container is an isolated environment holding an app and its dependencies. It shares the host OS kernel and contains only user-mode components, so it starts faster and uses less memory than a VM, which includes a full guest OS. The trade-off is weaker isolation than a VM, and the image must match the host OS type (Linux or Windows).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0066","card":"q-az-900-023-containers","sec":"az-900-s020","q":"What are the three pieces involved in running a container?","a":"<p>An image (packaged app, libraries and OS user-mode files), a repository or registry where images are stored, and a container runtime such as Docker that pulls the image and runs it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0067","card":"q-az-900-023-containers","sec":"az-900-s020","q":"Can you run a Windows container image on a Linux host?","a":"<p>Not directly, because containers use the host kernel. Linux images need a Linux kernel and Windows images need a Windows kernel (on Windows you can run Linux containers through a Linux VM layer such as WSL 2).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0068","card":"q-az-900-023-containers","sec":"az-900-s020","q":"What is the purpose of <code>docker run -d -p 8080:8080 &lt;image&gt;</code>?","a":"<p>It starts a container from the image in detached mode (<code>-d</code>, in the background) and maps host port 8080 to container port 8080 (<code>-p host:container</code>), so traffic to the host reaches the app.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0069","card":"q-az-900-024-compute-types","sec":"az-900-s021","q":"How do Azure VMs, Container Instances and Functions differ in management and billing?","a":"<p>VMs are IaaS: you manage OS, runtime and app, billed by VM uptime. ACI runs containers without VMs, you manage the app and image, billed by CPU and memory used. Functions are serverless: you provide code only, billed per execution on the Consumption plan.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0070","card":"q-az-900-024-compute-types","sec":"az-900-s021","q":"When would you use AKS instead of Azure Container Instances?","a":"<p>Use ACI for small, simple or short container workloads. Use AKS (managed Kubernetes) for large, long-running or multi-service workloads that need orchestration: scaling, rolling updates, self-healing and service discovery. AKS adds operational complexity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0071","card":"q-az-900-024-compute-types","sec":"az-900-s021","q":"What is a container group in ACI?","a":"<p>A set of containers that run on the same host and share lifecycle, network and storage, similar to a Kubernetes pod. Use it when a main app needs a sidecar such as a logger.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0072","card":"q-az-900-024-compute-types","sec":"az-900-s021","q":"What is an Azure Function triggered by, and what is a good use case?","a":"<p>A trigger such as an HTTP request, a timer, or a new blob or queue message. A good use case is event-driven work like resizing an image when it is uploaded. Poor fits are long-running, steady heavy workloads, where a VM or container is cheaper.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0073","card":"q-az-900-024-compute-types","sec":"az-900-s021","q":"In the Python v2 programming model for Azure Functions, what does <code>@app.route(route=\"hello\")</code> do on a function?","a":"<p>In a <code>func.FunctionApp()</code> app, the decorator registers the function as an HTTP-triggered endpoint at the <code>/api/hello</code> path. The function receives a <code>func.HttpRequest</code> and returns a <code>func.HttpResponse</code>. Version note: this decorator style is the Python v2 model, and the older v1 model used a <code>function.json</code> file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0074","card":"q-az-900-025-vm-options","sec":"az-900-s022","q":"What are availability sets, and how do fault domains and update domains differ?","a":"<p>An availability set spreads VMs across hardware inside one data center. A fault domain is a rack-like group sharing power and network, so it protects against hardware failure. An update domain is a group Azure reboots one at a time during maintenance, so not all VMs go down together.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0075","card":"q-az-900-025-vm-options","sec":"az-900-s022","q":"What does <code>--platform-fault-domain-count 2 --platform-update-domain-count 3</code> do in <code>az vm availability-set create</code>?","a":"<p>It sets the number of fault domains and update domains the set spreads VMs across. VMs added to the set are distributed over those domains in rotation. More update domains means fewer VMs are rebooted at once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0076","card":"q-az-900-025-vm-options","sec":"az-900-s022","q":"Availability set or availability zone: which do you choose?","a":"<p>Zones protect against whole data center failure and give a higher SLA, so prefer them where the region supports zones. Availability sets protect against rack failure and maintenance inside one data center and suit regions without zones. A VM cannot be in both at once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0077","card":"q-az-900-025-vm-options","sec":"az-900-s022","q":"What is Azure Virtual Desktop?","a":"<p>A cloud desktop and app virtualization service that streams Windows desktops and apps to users on Windows, macOS, iOS, iPadOS, Android and browsers, without local installation. Version note: previously called Windows Virtual Desktop.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0078","card":"q-az-900-026-resources-created-with-a-vm","sec":"az-900-s023","q":"Which resources does Azure create when you create a VM?","a":"<p>The VM, an OS disk, a network interface, a virtual network and subnet (unless you choose existing), a public IP address, a network security group, and an SSH key for Linux if you choose SSH. Optional items vary by settings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0079","card":"q-az-900-026-resources-created-with-a-vm","sec":"az-900-s023","q":"Why put all resources of a VM in one resource group?","a":"<p>They share a lifecycle, so deleting the group removes the VM and every related resource in one step. If you delete only the VM, the disk, NIC, public IP and NSG stay and keep costing money.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0080","card":"q-az-900-026-resources-created-with-a-vm","sec":"az-900-s023","q":"You deleted a VM but your bill is still growing. What do you check?","a":"<p>Orphaned resources: the OS and data disks, public IP addresses, network interfaces and snapshots, which are separate resources and are not removed with the VM by default. Delete them or the whole resource group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0081","card":"q-az-900-027-application-hosting","sec":"az-900-s024","q":"What is an App Service plan and why is it the unit of scaling?","a":"<p>The plan defines the VMs (size, count and features) that host your web apps. All apps in one plan share those VMs, and scaling the plan scales all apps in it. A higher tier gives more power and features such as autoscale and slots.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0082","card":"q-az-900-027-application-hosting","sec":"az-900-s024","q":"Do web app names in Azure App Service need to be unique, and why?","a":"<p>Yes, globally, because the name is the hostname <code>&lt;app-name&gt;.azurewebsites.net</code> in a shared DNS namespace.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0083","card":"q-az-900-027-application-hosting","sec":"az-900-s024","q":"What is the difference between scaling up, scaling out and autoscale in App Service?","a":"<p>Scale up moves the plan to a bigger tier (more CPU and memory). Scale out adds instances of the plan. Autoscale adds or removes instances automatically from a metric such as CPU or a schedule.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0084","card":"q-az-900-027-application-hosting","sec":"az-900-s024","q":"How do you add user sign-in to an App Service web app without writing auth code?","a":"<p>Turn on App Service authentication (Easy Auth) and add an identity provider such as Microsoft, Apple, Facebook, GitHub or Google. The platform handles the login flow and tokens in front of your code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0085","card":"q-az-900-028-application-hosting","sec":"az-900-s024","q":"What does AKS manage for you and what do you pay for?","a":"<p>AKS is managed Kubernetes. Azure runs the control plane (scheduling, scaling, API server), and you run worker nodes that host containers. You pay for the node compute (the control plane has a free tier, with a paid tier for an uptime SLA).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0086","card":"q-az-900-028-application-hosting","sec":"az-900-s024","q":"Compare App Service, AKS and VMs for hosting an app.","a":"<p>App Service: lowest management, built-in scaling, auth and slots, but limited to supported runtimes. AKS: high flexibility for microservices and containers, medium responsibility, orchestration handles scaling. VMs: full control and the highest responsibility, with manual scaling or VMSS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0087","card":"q-az-900-028-application-hosting","sec":"az-900-s024","q":"When should you choose AKS over App Service for a web app?","a":"<p>Choose AKS when you run many containerized microservices that need orchestration, custom networking or portability across Kubernetes. Choose App Service for a standard web app or API where you want the least operations work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0088","card":"q-az-900-028-application-hosting","sec":"az-900-s024","q":"What do <code>kubectl create deployment</code> and <code>kubectl expose deployment --type=LoadBalancer</code> do?","a":"<p>The first creates a Deployment that runs the requested number of pod replicas from an image. The second creates a Service of type LoadBalancer, which gets a public IP so outside traffic reaches the pods on the chosen port.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0089","card":"q-az-900-029-virtual-networking","sec":"az-900-s025","q":"What is an Azure virtual network, and how is its address space defined?","a":"<p>A VNet is your private network in Azure. You give it an address space in CIDR notation (for example <code>10.0.0.0/16</code>) and split it into subnets. Address spaces of VNets you want to peer or connect must not overlap.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0090","card":"q-az-900-029-virtual-networking","sec":"az-900-s025","q":"How would you design a three-tier application in a VNet?","a":"<p>Use separate subnets for the web, middle and data tiers. Only the web tier faces the internet, the middle tier takes traffic from the web tier, and the data tier accepts traffic only from the middle tier. Enforce this with network security groups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0091","card":"q-az-900-029-virtual-networking","sec":"az-900-s025","q":"What is VNet peering, and is peered traffic encrypted?","a":"<p>Peering connects two VNets over Microsoft's private backbone, not the public internet, with low latency. It works within a region or across regions (global peering). Peered traffic is not encrypted by default by the peering itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0092","card":"q-az-900-029-virtual-networking","sec":"az-900-s025","q":"Why do you create the peering in both directions with <code>az network vnet peering create</code>?","a":"<p>A peering link is one-way per VNet, so each VNet needs its own link to the other. If you create only one, traffic does not flow fully (the status stays Initiated rather than Connected).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0093","card":"q-az-900-029-virtual-networking","sec":"az-900-s025","q":"What does Azure DNS do, and what is the difference between public and private zones?","a":"<p>It hosts DNS records (A, CNAME, MX and others) on Azure's name servers. Public zones answer internet queries. Private zones resolve names only inside linked VNets and keep internal names off the internet.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0094","card":"q-az-900-030-virtual-networking","sec":"az-900-s025","q":"What is a VPN Gateway and what connection types does it support?","a":"<p>It sends encrypted traffic between an Azure VNet and another network over the internet. Types: VNet-to-VNet (two Azure VNets), Site-to-Site (on-premises network and VNet) and Point-to-Site (single device and VNet).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0095","card":"q-az-900-030-virtual-networking","sec":"az-900-s025","q":"What is ExpressRoute and how does it differ from a VPN Gateway?","a":"<p>ExpressRoute is a private dedicated circuit from your network through a connectivity provider to Microsoft's edge, so traffic never crosses the public internet. It offers higher bandwidth, more predictable latency and reliability, at higher cost and with a provider needed. A VPN Gateway is quicker to set up and cheaper but runs over the internet with lower throughput.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0096","card":"q-az-900-030-virtual-networking","sec":"az-900-s025","q":"When would you use VNet peering instead of a VPN Gateway between two Azure VNets?","a":"<p>Use peering for two Azure VNets: it is faster and cheaper, with no gateway or encryption overhead. Use a VPN Gateway when you need encrypted transit or connect to a non-Azure network.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0097","card":"q-az-900-030-virtual-networking","sec":"az-900-s025","q":"Why does a VPN gateway need a subnet named <code>GatewaySubnet</code>?","a":"<p>Azure deploys the gateway VMs into a subnet with that exact name, and it is reserved for them (do not put other resources there). Missing or misnamed subnets block gateway creation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0098","card":"q-az-900-030-virtual-networking","sec":"az-900-s025","q":"What is a Site-to-Site VPN versus Point-to-Site VPN, and which suits remote employees?","a":"<p>Site-to-Site links a whole on-premises network to a VNet through a VPN device. Point-to-Site links one client device (such as a laptop) to the VNet, so it suits individual remote employees.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0099","card":"q-az-900-031-public-vs-private-endpoints","sec":"az-900-s026","q":"What is the difference between a public endpoint and a private endpoint?","a":"<p>A public endpoint has an IP address reachable over the internet. A private endpoint has a private IP address inside your VNet, so it is reachable only from that network or networks connected to it. A resource can have both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0100","card":"q-az-900-031-public-vs-private-endpoints","sec":"az-900-s026","q":"How would you stop a storage account from being reachable from the internet?","a":"<p>Create a private endpoint in your VNet for the storage account, and turn off public network access (or restrict it with firewall rules). Clients then use the private IP through private DNS, and traffic stays on Microsoft's network.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0101","card":"q-az-900-031-public-vs-private-endpoints","sec":"az-900-s026","q":"How does a VM relate to public and private IP addresses?","a":"<p>A VM always gets a private IP from its subnet, and a public IP is a separate Azure resource you can attach to its NIC. Removing the public IP removes direct internet exposure without affecting private connectivity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0102","card":"q-az-900-032-storage-services","sec":"az-900-s027","q":"What are the three main Azure storage services and when do you use each?","a":"<p>Blob Storage holds unstructured objects such as images, video, backups and logs. Azure Files provides SMB file shares for shared files and lift-and-shift file servers. Managed Disks provide block storage attached to VMs for OS and data disks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0103","card":"q-az-900-032-storage-services","sec":"az-900-s027","q":"What are the three types of blobs?","a":"<p>Block blobs for general files such as images and documents. Append blobs for append-only data such as logs. Page blobs for random-access data such as VHD disks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0104","card":"q-az-900-032-storage-services","sec":"az-900-s027","q":"How do you choose between Blob Storage and Azure Files?","a":"<p>Choose Blob for object storage accessed by REST, SDKs or CLI, such as web content and backups. Choose Azure Files when apps or users need a mounted network drive over SMB with normal file paths and shared access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0105","card":"q-az-900-032-storage-services","sec":"az-900-s027","q":"Why add a separate data disk to a VM instead of storing data on the OS disk?","a":"<p>A data disk is independent: you can size it, choose its performance tier, detach and reattach it to another VM, and keep it if the OS disk is rebuilt. It also keeps application data separate from the OS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0106","card":"q-az-900-033-storage-services","sec":"az-900-s027","q":"What problem does Azure File Sync solve?","a":"<p>Azure Files can be slow over the internet for on-premises users. File Sync caches frequently used files on a local Windows server for local speed, while the full data stays in Azure. It also keeps several servers in sync with one Azure file share.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0107","card":"q-az-900-033-storage-services","sec":"az-900-s027","q":"What is a storage account, and what does it hold?","a":"<p>A storage account is the top-level namespace and billing container for Azure Storage data. It can hold blobs, file shares, queues and tables (depending on type) and carries settings such as redundancy and performance tier.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0108","card":"q-az-900-033-storage-services","sec":"az-900-s027","q":"Why must you protect the storage account key when mounting an Azure file share with SMB?","a":"<p>The key gives full access to everything in the account. Keep it out of scripts and source control, rotate it, and prefer identity-based access or limited SAS tokens. Also SMB on port 445 must be reachable, since some networks block it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0109","card":"q-az-900-034-blob-access-tiers","sec":"az-900-s028","q":"What are the Hot, Cool and Archive blob access tiers, and how do their costs compare?","a":"<p>Hot has the highest storage cost and lowest access cost, for frequently used data. Cool has lower storage and higher access cost, for infrequent access kept at least 30 days. Archive has the lowest storage cost and highest access cost, for rarely read data kept at least 180 days. Version note: Azure also added a Cold tier between Cool and Archive, with a 90-day minimum, so check the current docs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0110","card":"q-az-900-034-blob-access-tiers","sec":"az-900-s028","q":"What happens if you delete a blob or change its tier before the minimum retention of Cool or Archive?","a":"<p>You pay an early deletion charge, prorated for the remaining days. Moving to another tier counts as well as deleting. Plan the tier by how long the data really stays.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0111","card":"q-az-900-034-blob-access-tiers","sec":"az-900-s028","q":"Why can't you read an Archive blob right away, and how do you get access?","a":"<p>Archive data is offline. You must rehydrate it by changing its tier to Hot or Cool (or copying it to an online tier). This takes hours: standard priority can take up to 15 hours, and high priority is faster for small blobs at a higher price. Archive therefore does not suit data needing quick access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0112","card":"q-az-900-034-blob-access-tiers","sec":"az-900-s028","q":"A team stores compliance records that must be kept for years and are almost never read. Which tier and why?","a":"<p>Archive. Its storage price is lowest, and rare reads and the wait for rehydration are acceptable. If the records may be needed within hours, use Cool.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0113","card":"q-az-900-034-blob-access-tiers","sec":"az-900-s028","q":"What makes up the cost of Blob Storage besides the amount stored?","a":"<p>Operations (read, write, list) and data transfer, such as egress. Colder tiers cost more per operation, so a cheap tier can become expensive when read often.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0114","card":"q-az-900-035-redundancy","sec":"az-900-s029","q":"What are LRS, ZRS, GRS and GZRS in Azure Storage?","a":"<p>LRS keeps 3 copies in one data center. ZRS keeps 3 copies across 3 availability zones in one region. GRS keeps 3 LRS copies in the primary region plus 3 LRS copies in a distant paired region. GZRS keeps 3 ZRS copies in the primary region plus 3 LRS copies in the paired region.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0115","card":"q-az-900-035-redundancy","sec":"az-900-s029","q":"How do you choose among LRS, ZRS, GRS and GZRS?","a":"<p>LRS is cheapest and fine for data that is easy to recreate, but a data center loss can lose data. ZRS survives a zone or data center failure. GRS adds protection from a regional disaster. GZRS combines zone protection in the primary region with a second region, for the highest durability and availability at the highest cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0116","card":"q-az-900-035-redundancy","sec":"az-900-s029","q":"Does GRS let you read from the secondary region during normal operation?","a":"<p>Not by default. The secondary copy is used for failover. Read-access variants (RA-GRS and RA-GZRS) let applications read from the secondary endpoint at any time, which helps read availability during a primary outage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0117","card":"q-az-900-035-redundancy","sec":"az-900-s029","q":"Which redundancy options are for fault tolerance inside a region and which for disaster recovery?","a":"<p>LRS and ZRS stay in one region, so they give fault tolerance but no disaster recovery. GRS and GZRS copy to a second region, so they cover disaster recovery.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0118","card":"q-az-900-035-redundancy","sec":"az-900-s029","q":"How do you set redundancy when you create a storage account with the CLI?","a":"<p>Use the <code>--sku</code> parameter, for example <code>az storage account create --sku Standard_GRS</code> or <code>Standard_ZRS</code>. The SKU name encodes performance (Standard or Premium) and redundancy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0119","card":"q-az-900-036-storage-account-types","sec":"az-900-s030","q":"Which storage account type does Microsoft recommend for most workloads?","a":"<p>Standard general-purpose v2 (<code>--kind StorageV2</code>). It supports blobs, files, queues and tables, all redundancy options (LRS, ZRS, GRS, GZRS) and uses HDD-backed media.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0120","card":"q-az-900-036-storage-account-types","sec":"az-900-s030","q":"When would you use a Premium storage account instead of Standard?","a":"<p>When you need low latency and high, consistent throughput on SSD media. Premium types are specialized: Premium block blobs for block and append blobs, Premium file shares for Azure Files, and Premium page blobs for page blobs and VHDs. Redundancy options are narrower (mostly LRS and ZRS) and cost is higher.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0121","card":"q-az-900-036-storage-account-types","sec":"az-900-s030","q":"Which redundancy does a Premium page blob account support?","a":"<p>LRS and ZRS (ZRS availability depends on region), per current Azure docs as of 2025. Older AZ-900 material lists LRS only, so check the docs for your region.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0122","card":"q-az-900-037-moving-files","sec":"az-900-s031","q":"When do you choose AzCopy and when Storage Explorer?","a":"<p>AzCopy is a command-line tool for scripts, automation and bulk copies, including between storage accounts. Storage Explorer is a desktop GUI (Windows, macOS, Linux) for drag-and-drop transfers and for managing containers, tiers and SAS tokens.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0123","card":"q-az-900-037-moving-files","sec":"az-900-s031","q":"What is a shared access signature (SAS), and why use it?","a":"<p>A SAS is a token appended to a storage URL that grants limited access: chosen permissions (such as read and list), a start time and an expiry. You share access to specific data without giving out the account key, and the access ends automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0124","card":"q-az-900-037-moving-files","sec":"az-900-s031","q":"What is the difference between <code>azcopy copy</code> and <code>azcopy sync</code>?","a":"<p><code>azcopy copy</code> copies the files you specify, whether or not they exist at the destination. <code>azcopy sync</code> makes the destination match the source by copying only changes, and it can delete extra files at the destination when asked. Use sync for repeated updates of a folder.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0125","card":"q-az-900-037-moving-files","sec":"az-900-s031","q":"Why is a short expiry and minimal permissions a best practice for a SAS token?","a":"<p>A SAS works for anyone who holds it. Limiting permissions and lifetime limits damage if it leaks. For better control, use a user delegation SAS (signed with Entra ID credentials) or a stored access policy you can revoke.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0126","card":"q-az-900-037-moving-files","sec":"az-900-s031","q":"How does Azure File Sync differ from AzCopy for moving files?","a":"<p>AzCopy is a copy tool you run for a transfer. File Sync is an ongoing service: an agent on a Windows server keeps a local cache and an Azure file share in sync continuously, so on-premises users get local access speeds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0127","card":"q-az-900-038-migration-options","sec":"az-900-s032","q":"What are the three phases of an Azure Migrate project?","a":"<p>Discovery: an appliance collects metadata and performance data from servers and sends it to Azure. Assessment: you group servers and Azure recommends VM sizes, disk types and costs from the performance data. Migration: replicate and move the workloads, with test migrations first to find problems before cutover.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0128","card":"q-az-900-038-migration-options","sec":"az-900-s032","q":"Why run a test migration before the real one?","a":"<p>It starts a copy of the workload in Azure without affecting the source, so you catch networking, compatibility or sizing problems before cutover and reduce the risk of downtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0129","card":"q-az-900-038-migration-options","sec":"az-900-s032","q":"When would you use Azure Data Box rather than uploading data over the network?","a":"<p>When the data volume is too large for your bandwidth or the connection is limited, so transfer would take too long. Microsoft ships an encrypted device, you copy data to it, ship it back and Microsoft uploads it to your storage account. This is called offline transfer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0130","card":"q-az-900-038-migration-options","sec":"az-900-s032","q":"How do Data Box Disk, Data Box and Data Box Heavy differ?","a":"<p>Disk: up to about 35 TB on SSDs, the smallest. Data Box: up to about 80 TB in a ruggedized appliance. Heavy: up to about 1 PB, the largest. Capacities are as published at the time of writing and can change, so check the docs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0131","card":"q-az-900-038-migration-options","sec":"az-900-s032","q":"What security protections apply to Data Box devices?","a":"<p>Data is encrypted with AES (128-bit on Disk, 256-bit on Data Box and Heavy), the devices are tamper-resistant, and after import the disks are securely wiped to the NIST 800-88 R1 standard.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0132","card":"q-az-900-038-migration-options","sec":"az-900-s032","q":"Which tool do you pick for moving servers, files by script, files by GUI, and tens of terabytes with poor connectivity?","a":"<p>Servers, databases and web apps: Azure Migrate. Files by script: AzCopy. Files by GUI: Storage Explorer. Tens of terabytes with limited connectivity: Data Box Disk or Data Box, depending on size.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0133","card":"q-az-900-039-directory-services","sec":"az-900-s033","q":"What is the difference between authentication and authorization?","a":"<p>Authentication proves who or what is accessing (identity). Authorization decides what that identity is allowed to do. In Azure, Entra ID handles authentication and RBAC handles authorization of Azure resources.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0134","card":"q-az-900-039-directory-services","sec":"az-900-s033","q":"What is Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure AD), and what identity types does it hold?","a":"<p>It is a cloud identity service that authenticates and authorizes access to Azure, Microsoft 365, third-party SaaS and on-premises apps. It holds users, groups, service principals (apps) and managed identities. Naming note: the product was renamed from Azure AD, so both names appear in exams.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0135","card":"q-az-900-039-directory-services","sec":"az-900-s033","q":"What is a managed identity and why prefer it over storing credentials?","a":"<p>A managed identity is an identity Azure creates and manages for a resource such as a VM or web app. The resource gets tokens from Entra ID to call other services (Key Vault, Storage) with no password or secret in code. Credentials cannot leak or expire from your config.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0136","card":"q-az-900-039-directory-services","sec":"az-900-s033","q":"What is the difference between system-assigned and user-assigned managed identities?","a":"<p>A system-assigned identity is tied to one resource and deleted with it. A user-assigned identity is a separate resource you create once and attach to several resources, which helps when many resources need the same access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0137","card":"q-az-900-039-directory-services","sec":"az-900-s033","q":"What is a service principal, and when do you use one?","a":"<p>It is the identity of an application in a tenant, used by an app or automation to sign in and be given RBAC roles. Use it for apps and pipelines outside Azure, and prefer managed identities when the code runs on Azure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0138","card":"q-az-900-039-directory-services","sec":"az-900-s033","q":"When do you use Entra Domain Services instead of Entra ID?","a":"<p>Use Domain Services for legacy apps that need LDAP, Kerberos, NTLM, domain join or group policy, without running your own domain controllers. Use Entra ID for modern cloud apps that use OAuth, SAML and OpenID Connect.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0139","card":"q-az-900-040-authentication-methods","sec":"az-900-s034","q":"What is single sign-on (SSO) and how does Azure AD Connect extend it to on-premises directories?","a":"<p>SSO lets a user sign in once and reach many apps without more prompts. Microsoft Entra Connect (formerly Azure AD Connect) synchronizes on-premises Active Directory identities to Entra ID so one identity works in both. Naming note: the product has been renamed over time, so check the current name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0140","card":"q-az-900-040-authentication-methods","sec":"az-900-s034","q":"How do password hash synchronization and pass-through authentication differ?","a":"<p>With password hash sync, a hash of the on-premises password hash is stored in Entra ID and sign-in is checked in the cloud, so it works even if on-premises systems are down. With pass-through authentication, the sign-in is validated by an on-premises agent against Active Directory, so passwords stay on-premises but sign-in depends on the agent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0141","card":"q-az-900-040-authentication-methods","sec":"az-900-s034","q":"What are the three categories of authentication factors, and what is multi-factor authentication?","a":"<p>Something you know (password, PIN), something you have (phone, security key) and something you are (fingerprint, face). MFA requires two or more categories, so a stolen password alone is not enough. Two different passwords are not MFA.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0142","card":"q-az-900-040-authentication-methods","sec":"az-900-s034","q":"Is MFA free in Entra ID?","a":"<p>Version note: security defaults in the free tier enforce MFA registration for all users, and Conditional Access with granular MFA policies needs Entra ID P1 or higher licensing. Older material says MFA needs Premium. Check the current licensing page.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0143","card":"q-az-900-040-authentication-methods","sec":"az-900-s034","q":"What is passwordless authentication and which methods does Azure support?","a":"<p>It replaces the password with a stronger factor. Methods: FIDO2 security keys, Microsoft Authenticator (number match with biometrics or PIN), Windows Hello for Business, and certificate-based authentication. A Temporary Access Pass is a time-limited code for first sign-in or lost devices.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0144","card":"q-az-900-040-authentication-methods","sec":"az-900-s034","q":"Why is passwordless a good choice for users on mobile devices that can be lost?","a":"<p>There is no password to steal or guess, and the sign-in is tied to the device and a biometric or PIN. A lost phone alone does not give access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0145","card":"q-az-900-041-conditional-access","sec":"az-900-s035","q":"What is Conditional Access, and what are signals and decisions?","a":"<p>It is Entra ID's if-then policy engine. Signals are inputs such as user, group, location, device state, application and real-time sign-in risk. Decisions are outcomes: block access, allow, or allow with requirements such as MFA, a compliant device or an approved client app.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0146","card":"q-az-900-041-conditional-access","sec":"az-900-s035","q":"How would you protect corporate data on employees' personal devices (BYOD)?","a":"<p>Use Conditional Access to require an approved client app or a compliant (managed) device, and require MFA for sensitive apps. Access from unmanaged devices can be limited or blocked.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0147","card":"q-az-900-041-conditional-access","sec":"az-900-s035","q":"How would you require MFA only when users sign in from outside the office network?","a":"<p>Create a Conditional Access policy that applies to the users and apps, with a condition on location that includes any location and excludes trusted named locations, and grant control Require multifactor authentication. Test in report-only mode first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0148","card":"q-az-900-041-conditional-access","sec":"az-900-s035","q":"Why test a new Conditional Access policy in report-only mode?","a":"<p>A bad policy can lock out everyone, including admins. Report-only logs what would have happened without enforcing, so you can check the impact. Also keep emergency access accounts excluded.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0149","card":"q-az-900-042-rbac","sec":"az-900-s036","q":"What are the three elements of an Azure RBAC role assignment?","a":"<p>A security principal (who: user, group, service principal or managed identity), a role definition (what: a set of permissions) and a scope (where: management group, subscription, resource group or resource). Assignments at a higher scope are inherited by lower scopes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0150","card":"q-az-900-042-rbac","sec":"az-900-s036","q":"What is the difference between Owner, Contributor and Reader?","a":"<p>Owner has full access and can assign roles. Contributor has full access to manage resources but cannot assign roles. Reader can only view.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0151","card":"q-az-900-042-rbac","sec":"az-900-s036","q":"If a user has Reader at a resource group and Contributor at the subscription, what can they do in that resource group?","a":"<p>They can manage resources, because RBAC is additive: effective permissions are the union of all assignments, so the broader grant at the higher scope applies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0152","card":"q-az-900-042-rbac","sec":"az-900-s036","q":"How do you let a person manage web apps but nothing else?","a":"<p>Assign a narrow built-in role such as Website Contributor, scoped to the specific web app or resource group. If no built-in role fits, define a custom role with only the needed actions in <code>Actions</code> and limit it with <code>AssignableScopes</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0153","card":"q-az-900-042-rbac","sec":"az-900-s036","q":"What does the <code>--scope</code> parameter do in <code>az role assignment create</code>?","a":"<p>It sets where the role applies, as a resource ID path such as a subscription, resource group or resource. Use the narrowest scope that works, following least privilege.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0154","card":"q-az-900-042-rbac","sec":"az-900-s036","q":"What is the difference between Azure RBAC and Entra ID roles?","a":"<p>Azure RBAC controls access to Azure resources such as VMs and storage. Entra ID roles control access to directory tasks such as managing users and groups. They are separate systems.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0155","card":"q-az-900-043-zero-trust-and-defense-in-depth","sec":"az-900-s037","q":"What are the three Zero Trust principles and how do they map to Azure features?","a":"<p>Verify explicitly (MFA and strong authentication for each access), use least-privilege access (grant the minimum needed with RBAC and just-in-time access), and assume breach (limit blast radius, segment, monitor, and use Conditional Access).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0156","card":"q-az-900-043-zero-trust-and-defense-in-depth","sec":"az-900-s037","q":"What is defense in depth and how do you apply it in Azure?","a":"<p>Layered security, so a breach of one layer is stopped by the next. Layers from outer to inner: physical, identity and access, perimeter (DDoS protection, firewalls), network (NSGs, segmentation), compute (patching, endpoint protection), application and data (encryption at rest and in transit).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0157","card":"q-az-900-043-zero-trust-and-defense-in-depth","sec":"az-900-s037","q":"How does Zero Trust differ from the traditional perimeter security model?","a":"<p>The perimeter model trusts anything inside the corporate network. Zero Trust gives no implicit trust based on network location, so every request is verified for identity, device health and context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0158","card":"q-az-900-043-zero-trust-and-defense-in-depth","sec":"az-900-s037","q":"Which Azure features protect the network layer of defense in depth?","a":"<p>Network security groups, VNet segmentation, private endpoints, Azure Firewall and DDoS Protection at the perimeter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0159","card":"q-az-900-044-microsoft-defender-for-cloud","sec":"az-900-s038","q":"What does Microsoft Defender for Cloud do?","a":"<p>It is a cloud security posture management and workload protection tool for resources in Azure, on-premises and other clouds. It assesses configuration, gives a secure score and recommendations, tracks regulatory compliance and detects threats with alerts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0160","card":"q-az-900-044-microsoft-defender-for-cloud","sec":"az-900-s038","q":"What is the difference between CSPM and CWPP in Defender for Cloud?","a":"<p>Cloud security posture management (CSPM) finds misconfigurations and gives recommendations and a secure score. Cloud workload protection (CWPP) adds threat detection and protection plans for VMs, servers, storage, databases and apps, and these plans are paid.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0161","card":"q-az-900-044-microsoft-defender-for-cloud","sec":"az-900-s038","q":"How do Defender for Cloud and Azure Advisor differ?","a":"<p>Defender for Cloud focuses on security posture and threat protection. Advisor covers five categories (cost, security, reliability, operational excellence, performance) with best-practice advice. The security recommendations overlap, but Defender goes deeper on threats.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0162","card":"q-az-900-044-microsoft-defender-for-cloud","sec":"az-900-s038","q":"Why does the Defender for Cloud secure score matter?","a":"<p>It turns many recommendations into a single percentage, so you prioritize fixes that raise the score most and track progress.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0163","card":"q-az-900-045-what-affects-cost","sec":"az-900-s039","q":"What factors affect the cost of Azure resources?","a":"<p>Meters (what the resource bills for, such as operations or GB), the purchasing option (pay-as-you-go, reservations, spot, hybrid benefit), the region, and the size and tier of the resource. Data transfer out of Azure and storage redundancy also change cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0164","card":"q-az-900-045-what-affects-cost","sec":"az-900-s039","q":"What are Azure Reservations, Spot VMs and Azure Hybrid Benefit, and when do you use each?","a":"<p>Reservations: commit to 1 or 3 years for a steady workload and save up to about 72%. Spot VMs: unused capacity at up to about 90% off for interruptible work, where Azure can evict the VM. Hybrid Benefit: use existing Windows Server or SQL Server licenses to cut cost. Percentages are advertised maximums that change, so check the pricing page.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0165","card":"q-az-900-045-what-affects-cost","sec":"az-900-s039","q":"Why can the same VM cost different amounts in different regions?","a":"<p>Regional costs differ for power, network and facilities, plus local taxes and demand. Check the price in each candidate region, balanced against latency and data residency rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0166","card":"q-az-900-045-what-affects-cost","sec":"az-900-s039","q":"What is a poor use of Spot VMs?","a":"<p>A workload that cannot tolerate eviction, such as a production database. Use Spot for batch jobs, rendering, or dev and test work that can restart.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0167","card":"q-az-900-045-what-affects-cost","sec":"az-900-s039","q":"Why is VNet peering traffic within a region cheaper than across regions?","a":"<p>Peering bills per GB transferred, and cross-region (global) peering has a higher rate than same-region peering because it uses more backbone capacity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0168","card":"q-az-900-046-pricing-calculator-vs-tco-calculator","sec":"az-900-s040","q":"What is the difference between the Azure Pricing Calculator and the TCO Calculator?","a":"<p>The Pricing Calculator estimates the monthly cost of Azure resources you plan to deploy. The TCO Calculator compares the cost of running workloads on-premises with running them in Azure, to show savings from migrating.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0169","card":"q-az-900-046-pricing-calculator-vs-tco-calculator","sec":"az-900-s040","q":"Which calculator do you use to give a manager a cost estimate for a brand-new web app, and which to justify moving existing servers?","a":"<p>Pricing Calculator for the new app. TCO Calculator for the migration case, because it compares current on-premises costs with Azure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0170","card":"q-az-900-046-pricing-calculator-vs-tco-calculator","sec":"az-900-s040","q":"What are the three steps to use the TCO Calculator?","a":"<p>Define workloads (servers, databases, storage, networking), adjust assumptions (such as software assurance, electricity, IT labor, hardware costs), and view the report with the savings breakdown.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0171","card":"q-az-900-046-pricing-calculator-vs-tco-calculator","sec":"az-900-s040","q":"Which choices change a Pricing Calculator estimate?","a":"<p>Region, instance size, App Service tier, storage redundancy (LRS is cheaper than GZRS), Hybrid Benefit, reservation term and the number of operations. The result is an estimate, not a quote.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0172","card":"q-az-900-047-cost-management-and-billing-tool","sec":"az-900-s041","q":"What can you do with Microsoft Cost Management in the Azure portal?","a":"<p>Analyze spend by service, region, resource group or tag, see forecasts, create budgets and cost alerts, and review invoices and payments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0173","card":"q-az-900-047-cost-management-and-billing-tool","sec":"az-900-s041","q":"How do budgets and cost alerts work, and do they stop spending?","a":"<p>A budget sets an amount for a period with thresholds. When actual or forecast spend crosses a threshold, an alert notifies people or triggers an action group. By default a budget only notifies. You must use automation, for example an action group that runs a runbook, to shut resources down.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0174","card":"q-az-900-047-cost-management-and-billing-tool","sec":"az-900-s041","q":"How would you find out why this month's bill jumped?","a":"<p>Open Cost analysis, group by service, resource group, location or tag, compare with the previous period, and look for new or resized resources. Anomaly alerts can flag unusual spend automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0175","card":"q-az-900-048-tags","sec":"az-900-s042","q":"What are Azure tags and what are they used for?","a":"<p>Tags are name-value pairs on resources or resource groups, such as <code>department=marketing</code>. They filter portal views, group cost in Cost Management and invoices, and support automation and ownership tracking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0176","card":"q-az-900-048-tags","sec":"az-900-s042","q":"Do tags on a resource group automatically apply to its resources?","a":"<p>No. Tags are not inherited by default. To apply them to every resource, use Azure Policy (for example the Append or Modify effect, or a built-in inherit-tag policy).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0177","card":"q-az-900-048-tags","sec":"az-900-s042","q":"How do you require every resource to carry a department tag?","a":"<p>Assign an Azure Policy such as the built-in \"Require a tag on resources\" with the tag name. With the Deny effect, creation without the tag fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0178","card":"q-az-900-048-tags","sec":"az-900-s042","q":"How do you remove one tag using <code>az resource tag</code>?","a":"<p>The command replaces the whole tag set, so run it again listing only the tags you want to keep. Check the current behavior in the docs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0179","card":"q-az-900-049-10-governance-and-compliance","sec":"az-900-s043","q":"How do Blueprints, Policy and resource locks differ?","a":"<p>Blueprints package resource groups, ARM templates, policy and RBAC assignments for repeatable environments. Policy enforces rules on how resources are created and configured. Locks prevent changes or deletion of a resource regardless of role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0180","card":"q-az-900-049-10-governance-and-compliance","sec":"az-900-s043","q":"Which governance tool would you use to stop anyone creating VMs in a region you do not allow?","a":"<p>Azure Policy with the \"Allowed locations\" definition and the Deny effect. RBAC controls who can act, not where resources can go.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0181","card":"q-az-900-049-10-governance-and-compliance","sec":"az-900-s043","q":"How do Azure Policy and RBAC differ?","a":"<p>RBAC controls what actions a user can take. Policy controls what properties resources may have, regardless of who creates them, even an Owner.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0182","card":"q-az-900-050-azure-blueprints","sec":"az-900-s044","q":"What is Azure Blueprints and what is its workflow?","a":"<p>It is a way to define a repeatable set of governed resources: resource groups, ARM templates, policy assignments and RBAC assignments. Steps: create the blueprint with artifacts, publish a version, then assign it to a subscription, which deploys the artifacts. Version note: Microsoft has announced Blueprints is deprecated, with Template Specs and Deployment Stacks as the replacements, so check the current docs before adopting it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0183","card":"q-az-900-050-azure-blueprints","sec":"az-900-s044","q":"Why must you pick a target subscription when assigning a blueprint to a management group?","a":"<p>Management groups hold only subscriptions and other management groups, not resources, so the deployment needs a subscription to create resources in.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0184","card":"q-az-900-051-azure-policy","sec":"az-900-s045","q":"What is Azure Policy and when does it evaluate resources?","a":"<p>It is a service that enforces rules on resource properties, such as allowed regions, VM sizes or required tags. It evaluates at create and update time, and it scans existing resources periodically to report compliance, so existing non-compliant resources are flagged rather than deleted.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0185","card":"q-az-900-051-azure-policy","sec":"az-900-s045","q":"What do the effects Deny, Audit, Append, DeployIfNotExists and Disabled do?","a":"<p>Deny blocks the create or update. Audit logs a warning. Append adds properties such as a tag. DeployIfNotExists deploys a missing related resource, for example an agent or diagnostic setting. Disabled turns the policy off. AuditIfNotExists warns when a related resource is missing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0186","card":"q-az-900-051-azure-policy","sec":"az-900-s045","q":"Why start a new policy with Audit instead of Deny?","a":"<p>Audit shows what would be non-compliant without blocking work, so you see the impact first. Then switch to Deny once the results look right.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0187","card":"q-az-900-051-azure-policy","sec":"az-900-s045","q":"What is a policy definition versus a policy assignment?","a":"<p>A definition is the rule (condition and effect). An assignment applies a definition, with parameters, to a scope such as a management group, subscription or resource group. Child scopes inherit the assignment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0188","card":"q-az-900-051-azure-policy","sec":"az-900-s045","q":"In a custom policy rule, what do the <code>if</code> and <code>then</code> blocks do?","a":"<p>The <code>if</code> block holds the condition that matches resources (for example type equals a VM and size not in a list). The <code>then</code> block holds the effect to apply when it matches, such as deny.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0189","card":"q-az-900-052-resource-locks","sec":"az-900-s046","q":"What are the Azure resource lock types and how do they differ?","a":"<p>CanNotDelete (Delete) lets authorized users read and modify a resource but not delete it. ReadOnly allows reading only, so no modify and no delete.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0190","card":"q-az-900-052-resource-locks","sec":"az-900-s046","q":"How do locks differ from RBAC for preventing deletion?","a":"<p>A lock applies to every user, even Owners, until someone removes the lock. RBAC permissions depend on role, and roles can change. To remove a lock you need permission to manage locks (Owner or User Access Administrator).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0191","card":"q-az-900-052-resource-locks","sec":"az-900-s046","q":"What can go wrong with a ReadOnly lock?","a":"<p>It blocks operations that look like reads but are POST actions, for example listing storage account keys, and it can block a VM from being started or an app from scaling. Use CanNotDelete when you only want to prevent deletion.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0192","card":"q-az-900-052-resource-locks","sec":"az-900-s046","q":"Do locks apply to child resources?","a":"<p>Yes, a lock at a resource group or subscription is inherited by everything in it, including resources added later.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0193","card":"q-az-900-053-infrastructure-as-code-iac","sec":"az-900-s047","q":"What is Infrastructure as Code and why use it?","a":"<p>IaC defines infrastructure (networks, VMs, load balancers) in files you version and deploy by tool. It gives repeatable, consistent environments, fewer manual mistakes, code review, and easy recreation of environments. The trade-off is learning the language and managing state or drift.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0194","card":"q-az-900-053-infrastructure-as-code-iac","sec":"az-900-s047","q":"What is the difference between declarative and imperative IaC?","a":"<p>Declarative code states the desired end state (ARM templates, Bicep) and the platform decides how to reach it. Imperative scripts (CLI or PowerShell) list the steps to run in order. Declarative deployments are usually idempotent, so you can re-run them safely.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0195","card":"q-az-900-053-infrastructure-as-code-iac","sec":"az-900-s047","q":"What is Bicep and how does it relate to ARM templates?","a":"<p>Bicep is a simpler language for Azure resources that compiles to ARM JSON templates. It has the same capabilities with cleaner syntax and is deployed with the same <code>az deployment group create</code> command.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0196","card":"q-az-900-053-infrastructure-as-code-iac","sec":"az-900-s047","q":"Why does the same IaC definition give the same environment each time?","a":"<p>The definition is the single source of truth, and the platform makes the environment match it, like the same source code giving the same binary.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0197","card":"q-az-900-054-azure-portal","sec":"az-900-s048","q":"What is the Azure portal, and when would you choose it over CLI or PowerShell?","a":"<p>It is the web-based GUI for managing Azure. Use it for exploring, one-off tasks, visual monitoring and dashboards. For repeatable or automated work, use CLI, PowerShell or templates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0198","card":"q-az-900-054-azure-portal","sec":"az-900-s048","q":"What are Azure dashboards and where are they saved?","a":"<p>Dashboards are customizable pages of tiles showing live data from Azure Monitor and other services. They are saved in your Azure account (as a resource in a subscription), so they follow you to any device, and can be shared with RBAC.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0199","card":"q-az-900-055-command-line-tools","sec":"az-900-s049","q":"When do you choose Azure CLI and when Azure PowerShell?","a":"<p>Both are cross-platform and scriptable, and both call the same Azure APIs. Choose Azure CLI (<code>az</code>) if you work with Bash or on Linux and macOS and like JSON output. Choose Azure PowerShell (the Az module, cmdlets like <code>Get-AzResource</code>) if you know PowerShell and want to pipe objects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0200","card":"q-az-900-055-command-line-tools","sec":"az-900-s049","q":"How do you control output format in Azure CLI?","a":"<p>Use <code>--output</code> (<code>json</code>, <code>table</code>, <code>tsv</code>, <code>yaml</code>) and filter with <code>--query</code> using a JMESPath expression, for example <code>--query \"[].{Name:name}\"</code>. Default output is JSON.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0201","card":"q-az-900-055-command-line-tools","sec":"az-900-s049","q":"What does <code>az login</code> do and what does <code>az account set</code> change?","a":"<p><code>az login</code> authenticates you to Azure and loads your subscriptions. <code>az account set --subscription &lt;name-or-id&gt;</code> chooses the active subscription that later commands act on, so you do not create resources in the wrong one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0202","card":"q-az-900-056-azure-cloud-shell","sec":"az-900-s050","q":"What is Azure Cloud Shell, and what are its benefits?","a":"<p>A browser-based shell (Bash or PowerShell) with Azure CLI and PowerShell preinstalled and already authenticated. No local install is needed, and it is available from the portal, Azure docs and the mobile app.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0203","card":"q-az-900-056-azure-cloud-shell","sec":"az-900-s050","q":"Why does Cloud Shell need a storage account?","a":"<p>It mounts an Azure file share to keep your files, scripts and settings between sessions, because the shell host itself is temporary. Costs are small, but files in the share are billed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0204","card":"q-az-900-057-azure-arc","sec":"az-900-s051","q":"What is Azure Arc and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>Arc extends Azure management and governance (Policy, RBAC, tags, Monitor, Defender) to resources outside Azure, such as on-premises servers, other clouds and Kubernetes clusters. You manage a mixed estate from one control plane.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0205","card":"q-az-900-057-azure-arc","sec":"az-900-s051","q":"How do you bring a non-Azure server under Arc management?","a":"<p>Install the Azure Connected Machine agent on the server and connect it with <code>azcmagent connect</code>, giving the subscription, resource group, tenant and region. The server appears as an Azure resource with its own resource ID, so you can assign policy and tags to it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0206","card":"q-az-900-057-azure-arc","sec":"az-900-s051","q":"What can Arc-enabled Kubernetes enable on clusters outside Azure?","a":"<p>Arc-enabled data services (such as SQL Managed Instance and PostgreSQL) and application services (such as App Service, Functions and Logic Apps) can run on top of Arc-enabled Kubernetes. Some of these services were previews or changed over time, so check the current docs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0207","card":"q-az-900-057-azure-arc","sec":"az-900-s051","q":"Arc versus Azure Migrate: how do they differ?","a":"<p>Migrate moves workloads into Azure. Arc leaves the workload where it is and manages it with Azure tools.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0208","card":"q-az-900-058-azure-resource-manager-arm-and-arm-templ","sec":"az-900-s052","q":"What is Azure Resource Manager and why does every tool go through it?","a":"<p>ARM is the management layer that creates, updates and deletes all Azure resources. The portal, CLI, PowerShell, SDKs and templates all call the same ARM API, so authentication, RBAC, policy and locks apply consistently.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0209","card":"q-az-900-058-azure-resource-manager-arm-and-arm-templ","sec":"az-900-s052","q":"What does it mean that ARM is declarative?","a":"<p>You describe what you want (the desired resources and settings), not the steps. ARM figures out the order and creates or updates resources, so repeating a deployment is safe.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0210","card":"q-az-900-058-azure-resource-manager-arm-and-arm-templ","sec":"az-900-s052","q":"What are the main sections of an ARM template and what is each for?","a":"<p><code>$schema</code> (template language version), <code>contentVersion</code>, <code>parameters</code> (values supplied at deploy time), <code>variables</code> (reusable values), <code>resources</code> (what to deploy) and <code>outputs</code> (values returned).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0211","card":"q-az-900-058-azure-resource-manager-arm-and-arm-templ","sec":"az-900-s052","q":"Why use parameters in an ARM template instead of hard-coding values?","a":"<p>One template can then deploy many environments or regions by passing different values, such as <code>appName</code> and <code>location</code>, which keeps deployments consistent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0212","card":"q-az-900-059-azure-resource-manager-arm-and-arm-templ","sec":"az-900-s052","q":"How do you deploy an ARM template to a resource group with the CLI?","a":"<p><code>az deployment group create --resource-group &lt;rg&gt; --template-file azuredeploy.json --parameters key=value</code>. Use <code>az deployment group validate</code> first to check the template without deploying.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0213","card":"q-az-900-059-azure-resource-manager-arm-and-arm-templ","sec":"az-900-s052","q":"What does <code>dependsOn</code> do in an ARM template resource?","a":"<p>It tells ARM that this resource must be created after another one, for example a web app after its App Service plan. ARM deploys independent resources in parallel and uses <code>dependsOn</code> or references to order dependent ones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0214","card":"q-az-900-059-azure-resource-manager-arm-and-arm-templ","sec":"az-900-s052","q":"How do you capture an existing environment as a template?","a":"<p>Export the resource group with <code>az group export --name &lt;rg&gt;</code> or from the portal's Export template. The result may need cleanup before it is reusable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0215","card":"q-az-900-059-azure-resource-manager-arm-and-arm-templ","sec":"az-900-s052","q":"How would you deploy the same web app identically to several regions?","a":"<p>Write one parameterized ARM template, with parameters for app name and location, and run the deployment per region with different parameter values. The deployment is declarative, so each region ends in the same state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0216","card":"q-az-900-060-12-monitoring-tools","sec":"az-900-s053","q":"How do Azure Advisor, Service Health and Azure Monitor differ?","a":"<p>Advisor gives best-practice recommendations about your resources. Service Health reports Azure platform incidents and planned maintenance that affect you. Azure Monitor collects metrics and logs from your resources and applications and raises alerts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0217","card":"q-az-900-060-12-monitoring-tools","sec":"az-900-s053","q":"Which tool do you check first when your app is down and you suspect an Azure outage?","a":"<p>Azure Service Health, which shows incidents in the regions and services you use. Resource Health shows the health of a single resource. Then use Azure Monitor to inspect your own metrics and logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0218","card":"q-az-900-061-azure-advisor","sec":"az-900-s054","q":"What does Azure Advisor do and what are its recommendation categories?","a":"<p>It analyzes your resource configuration and usage and gives personalized best-practice recommendations in Cost, Security, Reliability, Operational Excellence and Performance. Each has affected resources and steps to fix.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0219","card":"q-az-900-061-azure-advisor","sec":"az-900-s054","q":"What is the Advisor score?","a":"<p>A percentage from 0 to 100 showing how well your resources follow best practices, per category, with a trend over time. Fixing high-impact recommendations raises it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0220","card":"q-az-900-061-azure-advisor","sec":"az-900-s054","q":"How would you find savings with Advisor?","a":"<p>Filter recommendations by the Cost category. Typical ones: shut down or resize underused VMs and buy reservations for steady usage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0221","card":"q-az-900-062-azure-service-health","sec":"az-900-s055","q":"What does Azure Service Health track?","a":"<p>Problems with Azure itself that affect your subscriptions and regions: service incidents (unplanned), planned maintenance and health advisories, plus a history with root cause analysis.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0222","card":"q-az-900-062-azure-service-health","sec":"az-900-s055","q":"How do you get alerted when an Azure outage hits the regions where you run workloads?","a":"<p>Create Service Health alerts with an action group (email, SMS, webhook) scoped to your subscriptions, services and regions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0223","card":"q-az-900-062-azure-service-health","sec":"az-900-s055","q":"Service Health versus Resource Health: what is the difference?","a":"<p>Service Health covers the Azure platform and regions broadly. Resource Health reports the status of an individual resource such as one VM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0224","card":"q-az-900-063-azure-monitor","sec":"az-900-s056","q":"What does Azure Monitor collect and what do you do with it?","a":"<p>It collects metrics (numeric time series such as CPU) and logs (event records) from Azure resources, apps and on-premises systems. You view charts, query logs, build dashboards, and set alerts that notify or trigger actions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0225","card":"q-az-900-063-azure-monitor","sec":"az-900-s056","q":"How do metrics and logs differ in Azure Monitor?","a":"<p>Metrics are lightweight numeric values at regular intervals, good for near real-time alerts and charts. Logs are rich event records stored in a Log Analytics workspace and queried with Kusto Query Language (KQL) for analysis and troubleshooting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0226","card":"q-az-900-063-azure-monitor","sec":"az-900-s056","q":"What is Application Insights and what does it track?","a":"<p>It is Azure Monitor's application performance monitoring. It tracks requests, response times, failed requests, dependencies, exceptions, availability and page views for web apps. Version note: it now stores data in a Log Analytics workspace (workspace-based), and the classic mode is retired.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0227","card":"q-az-900-063-azure-monitor","sec":"az-900-s056","q":"What does this KQL query do in Log Analytics?","a":"<p>It reads the Application Insights <code>requests</code> table, keeps failed requests from the last 24 hours, and counts them per hour and per operation name. Each <code>|</code> passes the result to the next step.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">KQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>requests\n| where success == false\n| where timestamp &gt; ago(24h)\n| summarize FailedCount = count() by bin(timestamp, 1h), name</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"az-900-f0228","card":"q-az-900-063-azure-monitor","sec":"az-900-s056","q":"How would you alert when a VM's CPU stays over 80%?","a":"<p>Create a metric alert rule on the VM's <code>Percentage CPU</code> metric with a condition (average over 80) over a window, such as 5 minutes, and attach an action group for notification.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0229","card":"q-az-900-064-dan-storage-identity-availability","sec":"az-900-s057","q":"An app stores many images, some used daily and some kept for regulatory reasons and rarely read. How do you store them cost-effectively?","a":"<p>Use Blob Storage with the Hot tier for active images and the Archive tier for regulatory records, or lifecycle management to move blobs between tiers by age. Hot has the lowest access cost, and Archive the lowest storage cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0230","card":"q-az-900-064-dan-storage-identity-availability","sec":"az-900-s057","q":"How do you keep four VMs running through a rack failure, a maintenance reboot, and a regional disaster?","a":"<p>Rack failure and maintenance: place VMs in an availability set (fault and update domains) or across availability zones. Regional disaster: deploy to both regions of a region pair and use GRS storage or Site Recovery.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0231","card":"q-az-900-064-dan-storage-identity-availability","sec":"az-900-s057","q":"Which migration option fits hundreds of gigabytes of images with a slow link?","a":"<p>A Data Box Disk, because Microsoft ships SSDs, you copy the data and send them back. For smaller sets or a fast link, AzCopy over the network is simpler.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0232","card":"q-az-900-065-will-pricing-performance-protection","sec":"az-900-s058","q":"Interns have Contributor rights but must not delete a production web app. What do you add?","a":"<p>A CanNotDelete resource lock on the app. It applies to every user whatever their role, even if roles change later.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"az-900-f0233","card":"q-az-900-066-will-pricing-performance-protection","sec":"az-900-s058","q":"Why use a metric alert on response time with Application Insights for a web app?","a":"<p>Application Insights collects request metrics automatically, so an alert (for example, average response time over 5 seconds) tells you about user-facing slowness before complaints arrive.</p>","hand":true}];
